  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 357 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nTODAY AND SATURDAY...\n\nEarly this morning surface high pressure was centered over \nnorthern Ontario with developing low pressure over northeast \nColorado. Light rain and a few thunderstorms were observed on \nregional radar over eastern South Dakota, southwest Minnesota, \nand northwest Iowa. The precipitation was associated with a 700\nmb trough and enhanced isentropic ascent ahead of the \ndeveloping low pressure system. Areas of fog developed over \nportions of northeast Minnesota since about 9 PM. High stratus \nstreaming northward ahead of the low pressure system is expected\nto limit additional cooling. Winds will also gradually increase\nthrough sunrise. The combination of these factors will allow \nfog to dissipate.\n\nThe rain will gradually spread northward through the day while \ncombating northerly dry air advection aloft. The dry air \nassociated with the anticyclonic circulation around the Ontario \nhigh pressure will limit the northward reach of rainfall today. \nForecast rain totals in our area of responsibility haven't \nchanged much from yesterday afternoon's forecast. There is still\n40% chance or more of 0.5-1.0 inches of rain south of a line \nfrom near Pine River to Sandstone to Hayward to near Phillips. \nMeanwhile the 40% chance of a tenth of an inch of rain wasn't \nmuch farther north indicating the tight gradient of rainfall \nexpected. That area was south of a line from near Park Rapids to\nnear Cromwell to near Glidden to near Woodruff. The distance \nbetween the 40% chance of a tenth of an inch and the 40% chance \nof 1 inch of rain are separated by only 40 miles!\n\nWinds over the southwest arm of Lake Superior will turn \nnortheasterly and strengthen today. The wind direction and \nresultant waves will pose a high risk of rip currents for the \nbeaches of the Twin Ports. A Beach Hazards Statement goes into \neffect at 1 PM. Rip current risk will diminish as winds relax \nthis evening and should no longer be a concern by 1 AM Saturday.\nWinds and waves build again on Saturday and the rip current \nrisk is expected to be high once again.\n\nBy 7 AM Saturday the high pressure over Ontario will propagate \neast into Quebec while the surface low over the Plains advances \ninto the Midwest. Rain will taper off during the morning \nSaturday except for a small area generally along and south of \nWI-70 in northwest Wisconsin.\n\nTemperatures today and Saturday will straddle normal. Highs \nwill be in the upper 50s to middle to upper 60s.\n\nSUNDAY THROUGH LATE NEXT WEEK...\n\nThe upper-level pattern will become less progressive Sunday \nthrough next week. A large area of high pressure will slowly \nmigrate eastward across the Canadian Prairies and northern \nOntario. Precipitation chances become negligible during the \nperiod. There are a few concerns with that evolution. Several \ndays of strong northeast winds over western Lake Superior will \nkeep a risk of rip currents in play for the beaches of the Twin \nPorts. Sunday appears to be the day with the highest waves. \nAreas of frost are possible each night through Wednesday. Monday\nnight may be the coldest of the stretch and there are portions \nof northeast Minnesota and northwest Wisconsin with a 20% chance\nof temperatures less than 32 degrees. As of this morning the \nforecast included clouds Sunday night which would limit frost \npotential. High temperatures will generally be near normal with \nvalues in the upper 50s to the mid and upper 60s.",
